artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a cosmological framework in which galaxies form within dark matter halos and, upon being accreted into a more massive host halo, become satellite galaxies. Each galaxy is associated with a hot gas reservoir that supplies cold gas for star formation through cooling. Observations indicate that:

(i) star-forming satellite galaxies form stars at rates comparable to central galaxies of the same stellar mass, implying that the transition from a star-forming to a passive state is rapid;
(ii) the bimodal distribution of specific star formation rates (sSFR) in galaxies, with relatively few galaxies at intermediate sSFR, must be reproduced by any successful model;
(iii) satellites must therefore sustain significant star formation for several Gyr after accretion, then quench on a comparatively short timescale.

Existing semi-analytic and hydrodynamical galaxy-formation models typically prescribe either instantaneous removal of the hot gas reservoir at infall or gradual ram-pressure / tidal stripping of that reservoir. Both prescriptions tend to produce satellite star-formation histories that decline too quickly after accretion and/or overpopulate the intermediate-sSFR (green-valley) region of the galaxy population.

Problem. Formulate and analyze a physically motivated model of gas processing in satellite galaxies—specifying the evolution of the hot gas reservoir, cold gas supply, star formation, and feedback after infall—such that the resulting population of satellites simultaneously:
(a) maintains star formation rates comparable to central galaxies of the same stellar mass for several Gyr after accretion;
(b) subsequently quenches on a timescale short enough that the predicted sSFR distribution reproduces the observed paucity of galaxies with intermediate specific star formation rates;
(c) reproduces the observed dependence of the satellite quenched fraction on stellar mass, halo mass, and time since infall.

Determine whether such a "delayed-then-rapid" quenching behavior can be achieved by relaxing the assumption of instantaneous hot-gas stripping alone, or whether additional physical processes (e.g. modified cooling, feedback regulation, stripping of cold gas) are necessary, and quantify the required parameters.

